  "nodes": [
    {
      "id": "9b1f1f8e-3c2a-4d55-91a7-6f0c2b7d4e10",
      "name": "Overview",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        -816,
        -416
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "color": 4,
        "width": 688,
        "height": 832,
        "content": "# AI newsletter\n\nReruns one Gluecrawl job per news site every Monday, keeps every article it has ever seen in an n8n data table, and asks an LLM to write a digest of only the stories that are new this week.\n\n## Setup\n\n1. **Install the node.** Settings → Community nodes → Install → `@gluecrawl/n8n-nodes-gluecrawl`.\n2. **Add credentials.** A Gluecrawl API key on the Gluecrawl nodes, an OpenAI key on *OpenAI Chat Model*.\n3. **Click *Set Up* once.** It creates the two data tables, then one Gluecrawl job per site. **Creating a job spends credits** — it runs the LLM mapper and is charged upfront — so point the three *Create … Job* nodes at the sites you actually want before clicking, and click only once.\n4. **Give the jobs a minute, then pick each one** in *Ars Technica AI*, *VentureBeat AI* and *The Verge AI*. Each field lists the jobs on your account; a job that is still mapping cannot be run yet.\n5. Run the Monday branch by hand once to watch it end to end, then publish.\n\n## Notes\n\n- **Reruns are the cheap path.** Creating a job runs the LLM mapper and is charged upfront. This workflow only ever starts a *run* on a job that already exists, which is billed for the pages it actually fetches — that is why job creation sits in setup and not on the schedule.\n- **Max Pages is 1, and that is deliberate.** These jobs open every article they list, so you are billed per article rather than per listing page. One index page per site per week is already more than a digest can use. Leaving the field empty does not mean \"use a default\": it means paginate until the site runs out.\n- **Gluecrawl allows 10 run starts per minute.** Past ten sources, split them across two schedules."
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "2c8d4a17-5e6b-4f39-8a20-1d7e3c9b5f42",
      "name": "Setup Note",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        -96,
        -416
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "color": 3,
        "width": 900,
        "height": 720,
        "content": "## 1 · Set up — click once\n\nCreates `ai_news_articles` and `ai_news_digests`, then one Gluecrawl job per site. The tables are reused if they already exist; **the jobs are not** — every click of this branch mints three more and charges for each.\n\nPoint the *Create … Job* nodes at whatever news index you like. The four columns they ask for are the ones the rest of the workflow reads, and `article_body` is the one that matters: it cannot be read off a listing card, so requesting it forces Gluecrawl to open each article and scrape the piece itself."
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "7f3b9c05-8d41-4e2a-b6f7-0c5a1e8d3b94",
      "name": "Scrape Note",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        -96,
        352
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "color": 5,
        "width": 908,
        "height": 840,
        "content": "## 2 · Read the week\n\nOne node per site, each holding a job created in setup. A run replays the mapper configuration Gluecrawl worked out back then, so no LLM runs at scrape time and every site comes back with the same four columns.\n\nA failed source does not sink the digest. *Scrape Each Source* retries three times, then continues past the broken site, and the brief is built only from rows that carry an article URL — so the week still gets written from whatever did come back.\n\nTo follow a fourth site, add a *Create … Job* node to setup, copy one of these three, wire it into *Merge Sources* and raise that node's Number of Inputs."
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "a5e2d738-1b94-4c60-8f3d-2a7c6b019e85",
      "name": "Store Note",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        848,
        528
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "color": 6,
        "width": 500,
        "height": 432,
        "content": "## 3 · Keep only what is new\n\nThe archive is the dedupe key: *Only New Articles* passes through the URLs the table has never seen, so a story that sits on the front page for three weeks is written up once. The first execution treats the whole page as new — expect one fat digest, then weekly deltas.\n\nA week with nothing new emits no items, so the LLM is never called and no digest row is written."
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "d4c71a96-0f38-4b25-9e7a-3b8d5f2c6a01",
      "name": "Write Note",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        1392,
        432
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "color": 7,
        "width": 760,
        "height": 680,
        "content": "## 4 · Write it up\n\nThe whole week reaches the model in one prompt, which is what lets it group related stories rather than summarise them one at a time. Each story carries an excerpt of its body, so the digest can report what happened rather than reword the headline.\n\n**Each site contributes its ten most recent stories**, and a capped section says so in its heading. A news index holds several weeks of articles, so without that limit the first edition would be enormous and every edition would be expensive. Change `PER_SOURCE` in *Build the Brief* to trade cost against coverage — the archive keeps everything either way.\n\nThe digest is stored as a row. To have it delivered instead, put a Gmail, Slack or Ghost node after *Write the Digest* — `{{ $json.text }}` is the article."
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    },
